{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"naive-bayes","title":"Text Classification with Naive Bayes","group":"Machine Learning","order":13,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Classify documents by their words: turn each text into word counts, then let Naive Bayes estimate which class makes those words most likely.</p>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Vectorise</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>CountVectorizer</code> builds a vocabulary and turns each document into a vector of word counts (bag of words).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Train</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>MultinomialNB</code> learns how often each word appears in each class.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Predict</div><div class=\"step-desc\">For a new text it picks the class with the highest probability, assuming words are independent — the “naive” part.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB\n\ntexts = [\"the team won the cricket match\", \"stocks fell as markets closed lower\",\n         \"a great goal in the final\", \"the bank raised interest rates\",\n         \"fans cheered the winning shot\", \"investors sold shares today\"]\nlabels = [\"sports\", \"finance\", \"sports\", \"finance\", \"sports\", \"finance\"]\n\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer()\nX = vectorizer.fit_transform(texts)          # sparse matrix: 6 documents × vocabulary\nprint(X.shape)                               # (6, 27)\n\nmodel = MultinomialNB().fit(X, labels)\nnew = vectorizer.transform([\"the match was a great win\", \"interest rates and shares\"])\nprint(model.predict(new).tolist())           # ['sports', 'finance']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Call <code>fit_transform</code> on training text only, then <code>transform</code> on new text — otherwise the vocabulary (and the columns) change between training and prediction.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>The notebook's real dataset</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from sklearn.datasets import fetch_20newsgroups\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn import metrics\n\ndata = fetch_20newsgroups(subset=\"all\")                  # ~18,800 posts in 20 topics (downloads once)\nX = CountVectorizer().fit_transform(data.data)\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, data.target, test_size=0.3, random_state=0)\nmodel = MultinomialNB().fit(X_train, y_train)\ny_pred = model.predict(X_test)\nprint(f\"Accuracy: {metrics.accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred):.2f}\")   # 0.85 in the notebook run\nprint(metrics.classification_report(y_test, y_pred, target_names=data.target_names))</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The report shows per-class precision and recall. In the notebook, <code>comp.os.ms-windows.misc</code> had precision 0.97 but recall 0.25 — the model was rarely wrong when it chose that class, but missed most of its posts. Accuracy alone would hide that.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Why Naive Bayes for text</h3><p>Fast, works with little data, handles huge vocabularies — a strong baseline for spam filters and topic tagging.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Improving it</h3><p><code>TfidfVectorizer</code> (down-weights common words), removing stop words, bigrams (<code>ngram_range=(1, 2)</code>).</p></div></div></section>"}